+# OneDeck Knowledge and Automation Constitution v1.0
+
+This constitution is the governing product contract for OneDeck. Lower-level engine, UI, compiler, and acceptance contracts must be interpreted consistently with it. It does not replace the Comprehensive Rules or the technical contracts; it defines the authority, knowledge, automation, and recovery boundaries within which they evolve.
+
+<!-- clause: CONST-AUTH-001 -->
+The Magic Comprehensive Rules are the authority for rules meaning. The real game as performed by the humans is the authority for observed game facts. `GameState` is a fallible digital representation of that reality and must never be treated as more authoritative than the game it represents.
+
+<!-- clause: CONST-KNOW-001 -->
+OneDeck may act on facts that are explicitly represented, deterministically derived from represented facts, or recognized by a validated implementation. It must not silently invent an unknown target, choice, event, object state, or rules outcome. When required knowledge is absent or ambiguous, OneDeck exposes that boundary honestly and leaves the unresolved meaning to the human.
+
+<!-- clause: CONST-KNOW-002 -->
+Knowledge is provenance-bearing. State facts, action facts, semantic events, and derived rule facts remain distinguishable enough to explain why OneDeck believes a relevant fact is true. A later object or presentation cue must not retroactively fabricate the provenance of an earlier fact.
+
+<!-- clause: CONST-AUTO-001 -->
+Automation is selected by product value and implementation cost, not by a mandatory maturity ladder. Manual, guided, deterministic recognizer, finite evaluator, Oracle compiler, and card-specific implementations are alternative mechanisms. `manual -> guided -> auto -> general rules engine` is not a required progression, and a General Rules Engine is a separate architecture choice rather than a target level.
+
+<!-- clause: CONST-AUTO-002 -->
+High-ROI automation is permitted when its behavior is sufficiently deterministic, testable, and recoverable. The fact that automation can be wrong does not by itself prohibit useful automation; reversibility and correction are part of its safety model. Frequent human confirmation is a signal to evaluate that domain as a future automation investment rather than a reason to add confirmation indefinitely.
+
+<!-- clause: CONST-AUTO-003 -->
+For triggers and comparable semantic recognition, OneDeck automatically creates pending semantic work only when it can safely determine that the event occurred under the represented knowledge. Otherwise the human remains responsible for asserting or resolving the missing meaning. OneDeck should automate cheap, common negative determinations when reliable—for example, recognizing that an ordinary spell has no supported cast-time effect—without requiring universal card-specific guided UI.
+
+<!-- clause: CONST-UI-001 -->
+OneDeck does not require card-specific generated choice UI as a product goal. Structured prompts such as target, mode, or X selection may exist where their implementation has justified value, but the architecture must not assume that every card can or should be decomposed into such UI. Honest manual resolution remains a first-class path.
+
+<!-- clause: CONST-RECOVERY-001 -->
+Every automated semantic mutation must preserve a comprehensible recovery path. Undo and correction are automation-safety mechanisms, not convenience-only features. A user must be able to recover from an incorrect automated interpretation without corrupting unrelated game history.
+
+<!-- clause: CONST-RECOVERY-002 -->
+When digital state and real game reality diverge, correction toward the observed reality is legitimate even when the divergence originated outside OneDeck. Recovery must preserve deterministic state history where practical and must not claim that OneDeck's previous interpretation was physically authoritative.
+
+<!-- clause: CONST-EVOLVE-001 -->
+Existing automation may be retained, expanded, reduced, or replaced according to measured friction, frequency, correctness risk, implementation cost, and recovery quality. The constitution forbids architectural drift toward universal rules automation by assumption; expansion requires an explicit product and evidence rationale.

